Create a file you can share, print or read outside Skribisto.

## Export your writing

1. Press `Ctrl+Shift+E` to open Export.
2. Under *What to export*, check the chapters or scenes you want to include.
   Checking a chapter selects its scenes too.
3. Choose a *Format*. Use Word or LibreOffice for editing and comments, PDF for printing,
   or EPUB for an ebook reader.
4. Choose a *Style* for the layout.
5. Use the folder button beside *Destination* to choose a file name and where to save it.
6. Click *Export*. When it finishes, click *Open* or *Show in folder* in the message.

You can cancel while the export runs. If the destination file already exists, Skribisto asks
before replacing it.

## Export just one chapter or book

Open the item, then choose *Work ▸ Export*. The submenu offers commands such as *Export Book*
or *Export Chapter*, with that item already selected. Use *Choose…* for a custom selection.

Items with *Include in exports* turned off in the Inspector are hidden from the selection.
*Show non-exportable* reveals them. Selecting one includes it for this export only.

## Choose a layout

A *Style* controls headings, scene breaks, line spacing, page breaks and which extra content
is included, such as synopses, notes and comments. Some formats support more layout options
than others.

To change a built-in style, first make a copy: open
*Settings ▸ Compile & Export ▸ Export Formats*, select the style and choose *Duplicate*.
Then choose *Edit* on the copy.

Other available formats are HTML, Markdown, Djot, Text and LaTeX.

## Send a manuscript for feedback

Word (`.docx`) and LibreOffice (`.odt`) can include comments. In the export style, turn on
*Include comments* and leave *Include round-trip markers* on so Skribisto can match returned
chapters to your project.

See [bringing back edits and comments](:help-round-trip) for the full process.
